WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.030
Film Courage: I think you’ve mentioned that
you enjoy the word ‘Why?’

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:03.620
Karl Iglesias: It’s my favorite word.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.700
Film Courage: Favorite word.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:05.700
Okay.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:11.980
How should a writer incorporate the word Why
and that never-ending curiosity from the minute

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.450
they start writing?

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:18.410
Karl Iglesias: Well…that’s a great question!

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.750
I mean Why is connected to curiosity.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:26.890
I think all great artists are highly curious
individuals.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:34.899
For me I’m just curious about everything
so the reason I love writing so much is because

00:00:34.899 --> 00:00:39.420
you know just like learning about something
I mean…I’m not the first one to say this

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.610
but I write about things that fascinate me
because I don’t know about them.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:52.640
In other words I write in order to learn more
about a topic and answer that Why question.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.410
Why is this done?

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.910
How or whatever?

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:05.750
But the Why (in terms of writing) connects
to the reason why characters do what they

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.750
do.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.729
That’s what audiences really want to know
and that comes to motivation.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:13.140
So what is really important.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.130
It’s a cliché actor’s question.

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.290
“Like what’s my motivation?

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.290
Why am I doing this?

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.210
Why am I saying this in a scene?”

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:24.770
Well when you’re writing a story and you’re
creating a character and you have all these

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.070
things happening in the story that you’ve
come up with okay…he’s going to do this…he’s

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.000
going to do that…and that is going to happen.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.810
You’ve got to know why.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:37.270
Audiences really want to know why because
if it doesn’t connect to a universal motivation,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.630
it’s going to feel contrived and fake and
that happens all the time.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.450
He says “Aahh, you know what?

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.159
That character wouldn’t do that.”

00:01:45.159 --> 00:01:49.909
And you’ll be true about that.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:52.310
So for me Why connects to motivation.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.890
Once I know what a character wants, the next
question in Why.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:01.590
And you better have a good answer to why a
character wants what they want.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:05.890
Why did they react a certain way (which creates
the plot)?

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.060
It all comes through character.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.410
It’s all psychology, you know?

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:13.500
Film Courage: Have you ever seen a writer
whose curiosity could be ramped-up a couple

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.660
notches [increased]?

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.030
You know there are certain people who don’t
want to admit to being wrong or not knowing

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.370
something and they need a little more of that
[curiosity].

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.730
Karl Iglesias: Of course.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:30.000
I mean it’s just like any type of character
or human that you meet.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.140
I mean there are people who are very curious
and there are people who are totally non-curious,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.960
you know?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:39.720
And you can tell just by people who ask questions.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:43.090
Usually people who ask questions…I mean
when I meet somebody new, I constantly ask

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.090
questions.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.090
I want to know everything, you know?

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:49.190
I want to know everything about a person,
so I’m constantly asking questions.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:55.460
But if somebody asks you questions to, that
shows you their curiosity.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.080
Curiosity is not a trait that is in every
human being, although I feel it should be.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:03.670
They’re curious about certain things and
they are less curious about others.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.610
Depends on the human being really.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.000
Film Courage: Well how does that [curiosity]
show up in the writing?

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.500
Not that they’re not curious but that…

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.500
Karl Iglesias: I think it depends.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:17.520
I think it goes to how original the writing
is.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:28.390
How much of a world is explored because somebody
who is not really curious about something,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:34.140
it will be flat because you only get just
a certain level material, right?

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.470
Whether it’s one-dimensional characters,
right?

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:42.110
Where it’s like one surface of a character
because you didn’t go deeper as the research

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:48.900
or knowing about that character or their psychology.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:57.520
I feel when you come across stories that are
really rich you can tell the writer was very

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.800
curious because they dug deep enough and that
is what they explored.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.580
Film Courage: So SILENCE OF THE LAMBS?

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.920
Karl Iglesias: Perfect example.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.920
I mean look at all the richness of detailed
character psychology.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.550
Even that world.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:18.910
THE GODFATHER perfect example.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.010
You can tell the artists that are really curious
about the world they’re writing in and the

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:24.010
characters.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:32.680
Those who write truly three-dimensional characters
and great complex relationships.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.250
So that is how it affects it.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.690
And not everybody is curious, so that is why
you have good writers and bad writers.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:37.880
Question for the Viewers: Does this video
make you more curious than you already are?

